  "text": "Radio waves, microwaves, visible light, X-rays and gamma rays are the same thing at different speeds of wobble. They feel like completely different phenomena because of what they do to matter. Slow wobbles are long waves. They go around obstacles and through walls. Speed it up and the waves get short enough to shake water molecules, which is a microwave oven. Keep going and you reach one absurdly narrow band, and that is everything you have ever seen. Faster still and each wave carries enough energy to knock electrons off atoms. That is an X-ray. There is no boundary anywhere along it. The names are just labels we put on a ramp. So why do we see that particular slice and not another? Because it is the band that gets through water and air best, and our eyes started in the sea. Your entire sense of what the world looks like is a bandwidth accident. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
